#!/bin/sh
#
# haiku-provision-native-builder -- turn a freshly-booted DeBeOS (Haiku) arm64
# box into a working native package builder, using ONLY the DeBeOS package repo
# plus a git clone of the ports tree. Run this ON the box (over SSH), not on a
# workstation.
#
# Why this exists: native package builds must run on Haiku arm64 -- haikuporter
# refuses to build ports on a Linux host ("Native building not supported on this
# platform"). A stock canonical image is deliberately lean, so it lacks the
# toolchain, the ports tree, and a couple of environmental things haikuporter
# assumes are present. This script installs all of that from the repo the image
# is already configured for, so any instance launched from the canonical AMI
# becomes a builder with one command -- no dependence on a pre-seeded golden
# guest.
#
# What it does, and why each step is load-bearing (all learned by getting a real
# `jq` built end-to-end on a lean box):
#
#   1. Installs the build toolchain from the repo. gcc/binutils/make and the
#      autotools are obvious; the less-obvious ones are:
#        - python3.14  -- haikuporter is Python and unpacks .tar.gz via the
#          stdlib tarfile module, which needs the zlib extension. The repo's
#          python3.14 HAS zlib built in; an older seeded python3.10 did not, and
#          that (not any real fetch problem) is what made tarfile reject a valid
#          tarball as "Unrecognized archive type". We verify zlib explicitly and
#          ABORT if it is missing rather than fail cryptically mid-build.
#        - bison/flex/m4/patch/gawk/diffutils -- individual ports pull these in
#          as build-requires (jq needed bison); install the common set up front.
#        - haiku_devel -- the C-runtime glue + private headers ports link against.
#        - git/gzip/xz_utils -- the lean base ships none of these, and the ports
#          workflow needs all three (clone the tree, unpack tarballs).
#        - libxml2 -- provides `cmd:xmlcatalog`, needed via docbook_xml_dtd.
#        - gettext -- the TOOLS package (not the gettext_libintl runtime lib):
#          carries `cmd:autopoint` and the AM_GNU_GETTEXT*/AC_LIB_* autoconf
#          macros. Without it, `autoreconf` on any AM_GNU_GETTEXT-using port
#          fails; ~11 campaign-#136 ports died at bootstrap for this (issue #173).
#
#   1b. Overlays the C89-safe gnu/sched.h + gnu/crypt.h (`inline` -> `__inline`,
#      graviton cc9e418fbf / #171) INTO the activated haiku_devel hpkg, so ports
#      built in strict C89 mode (argon2, opusfile) stop failing to parse those
#      compatibility headers. This is a STOPGAP overlay onto the sysroot the
#      canonical AMI baked; the clean path is #171 flowing into the next canonical
#      bake, after which it is a no-op. See the section 1b block below for why the
#      fix must land inside the hpkg (haikuporter's chroot mounts a packagefs from
#      the hpkg files) rather than as a loose file on the host.
#
# On resolving build-prerequisites in general: haikuporter resolves a recipe's
# BUILD_PREREQUIRES only against LOCAL pools (/boot/home/haikuports/packages and
# /boot/system/packages), never against the remote DeBeOS repo -- so a
# prerequisite that is published and one `pkgman install` away still fails hard
# ("build-prerequires cmd:xmlcatalog ... could not be resolved") and takes the
# whole chain with it. Do NOT chase these by growing the list above: that is
# whack-a-mole, and `haiku-nativebuild` already closes the loop properly by
# parsing every unresolved resolvable out of the build log and `pkgman install`ing
# it (pkgman accepts `cmd:`/`devel:` expressions directly) until the set stops
# shrinking. Build ports THROUGH haiku-nativebuild, not by calling haikuporter
# directly. The entries above are only a warm start.
#
#   2. Clones the ports tree and haikuporter itself over HTTPS. TLS works on the
#      canonical image (openssl3 + a CA store are in the base), so a plain
#      `git clone` of both is enough -- there is no need to relay haikuporter
#      from anywhere. Writes a minimal haikuports.conf targeting arm64.
#
#   3. Creates /boot/system/settings/fonts. haikuporter's chroot setup does an
#      unconditional `cp -r /system/settings/fonts ...` under `set -e`; the lean
#      image does not create that directory, so the copy fails and aborts the
#      build BEFORE the recipe even runs. An empty dir is enough.
#
#   4. Installs matching wget AND curl shims that both delegate to a shared
#      fetch helper (haiku-srccache-fetch). Sources are fetched two ways:
#      haikuporter's own downloader shells out to wget, but a number of recipes
#      (and haikuporter builds that prefer it) fetch with curl instead -- and
#      some upstreams (notably the github/gnome/tukaani/invisible-island tarball
#      CDNs) stall or hang when fetched from a Haiku box. A wget shim alone is
#      not enough: the curl path sails straight past it and re-downloads even
#      when the tarball is already seeded. So both fetchers are shimmed, and both
#      hand the (url, output) to one helper that fetches in a fixed order:
#        local seed dir  ->  shared S3 cache  ->  upstream (then store to S3).
#      The seed dir is a hand-dropped override (fetched on a fast host) for a
#      flaky upstream. The S3 tier (issue #162) is the shared, persistent cache:
#      on the native-EC2 spot fleet each ephemeral builder's LOCAL cache dies
#      with it, so without it N builders x successive waves re-download the same
#      tarballs. When SRCCACHE_S3 is set (the bake passes the work bucket), a
#      fetch checks S3 first and serves on hit; on a miss it fetches upstream,
#      verifies the transfer, renames into place, and only THEN stores the
#      verified file to S3 -- a truncated/failed fetch caches nothing, so a
#      poisoned entry can never hand a later build a false checksum mismatch. The
#      correctness discipline (atomic temp+rename, non-empty/short-read guard,
#      per-fetch hit/miss logging) is carried over from haiku-source-proxy, the
#      retired metal->QEMU-guest variant (graviton/scripts/haiku-wget-shim) that
#      relayed through a host proxy instead. Haiku has no /usr/bin/env and SSM
#      exports an empty PATH, so the helper and shims run curl, python and the
#      SSM agent by ABSOLUTE path resolved here at provision time, and use
#      `debeos-ssm-agent s3 cp` for S3 (the box has no awscli).
#
# After this runs, build a port with:
#     cd "$HP_DIR" && python3.14 ./haikuporter --config="$CONF" -y \
#         --all-dependencies <port>
# (build a port's own build-requires first; `--all-dependencies` covers them,
# but a bare `-G` does not.) Then publish the resulting .hpkg to the DeBeOS repo
# from a Haiku host with graviton/scripts/haiku-repo-add.
#
# Everything is idempotent: re-running skips what is already present.
#
# Config (env only -- NO account/host names are baked into this tracked file):
#   HP_HOME      home dir to install under      (default /boot/home)
#   PORTS_REPO   ports tree git URL             (default the public HaikuPorts tree)
#   HP_REPO      haikuporter git URL            (default the public HaikuPorts haikuporter)
#   TARGET_ARCH  packaging target architecture  (default arm64)
#   PACKAGER     PACKAGER line for haikuports.conf (default a generic DeBeOS id)
#   SEED_DIR     wget/curl-shim seed directory  (default $HP_HOME/seed)
#   SRCCACHE_S3  shared S3 source-tarball cache prefix, e.g.
#                s3://haiku-graviton-<acct>-<region>/srccache (issue #162).
#                Empty (default) = S3 tier off; shims do seed -> upstream only.
#                No account/host names are baked into this tracked file: the bake
#                (haiku-bake-builder) resolves the work bucket and passes it here.
#   DOWNLOAD_MIRROR  DeBeOS-owned HaikuPorts source mirror, e.g.
#                https://sources.debene.dev (issue #172). haikuporter DEFAULTS its
#                DOWNLOAD_MIRROR to https://ports-mirror.haiku-os.org, which is now
#                permanently NXDOMAIN -- so any port whose primary upstream has
#                rotated fails source fetch with no working fallback. Setting this
#                writes a DOWNLOAD_MIRROR line into haikuports.conf so the fallback
#                URI points at the DeBeOS mirror instead. Empty (default) = leave
#                the line out (haikuporter keeps its dead default). The mirror is
#                populated from verified fetches -- see haiku-mirror-sync and the
#                mirror-promote step in haiku-nativebuild. Passed by the bake.
#   OVERLAY_DIR  local path to graviton/haikuports-patches, if it is already on
#                this box (default $HP_HOME/haikuports-patches). When that path
#                has no recipes/ subdir, the overlay is fetched from OVERLAY_REPO
#                instead. Issue 764.
#   OVERLAY_APPLY  local path to haiku-ports-overlay-apply (default
#                $HP_HOME/haiku-ports-overlay-apply). Fetched with the overlay
#                when absent. haiku-bake-builder stages both here already, so a
#                baked builder re-provisioning uses its own copies.
#   OVERLAY_REPO DeBeOS repo to fetch the overlay from when it is not already on
#                the box (default the public DeBeOS repo). Needs no credentials.
#   OVERLAY_REF  branch/tag to fetch the overlay from (default graviton).
#   OVERLAY_SKIP 1 = do NOT apply the recipe overlay. Logged as a WARNING,
#                never the default: a builder without the overlay re-fails ports
#                this project has already fixed (issue 764).
#
set -eu

HP_HOME="${HP_HOME:-/boot/home}"
PORTS_REPO="${PORTS_REPO:-https://github.com/haikuports/haikuports.git}"
HP_REPO="${HP_REPO:-https://github.com/haikuports/haikuporter.git}"
TARGET_ARCH="${TARGET_ARCH:-arm64}"
PACKAGER="${PACKAGER:-DeBeOS <debeos@example.invalid>}"
SEED_DIR="${SEED_DIR:-$HP_HOME/seed}"
SRCCACHE_S3="${SRCCACHE_S3:-}"
DOWNLOAD_MIRROR="${DOWNLOAD_MIRROR:-}"
OVERLAY_DIR="${OVERLAY_DIR:-$HP_HOME/haikuports-patches}"
OVERLAY_APPLY="${OVERLAY_APPLY:-$HP_HOME/haiku-ports-overlay-apply}"
OVERLAY_REPO="${OVERLAY_REPO:-https://github.com/felipedbene/Haiku-Graviton.git}"
OVERLAY_REF="${OVERLAY_REF:-graviton}"
OVERLAY_SKIP="${OVERLAY_SKIP:-0}"

PORTS_DIR="$HP_HOME/haikuports"
HP_DIR="$HP_HOME/haikuporter"
CONF="$HP_HOME/haikuports.conf"
SHIM_DIR="$HP_HOME/config/non-packaged/bin"
# The GCC package ships the arm64 cross-triplet compilers
# (aarch64-unknown-haiku-g++/-gcc/-c++) ONLY here, not under /boot/system/bin.
# GNU make's built-in default compilers on arm64 are exactly those triplet
# names, so raw-Makefile ports (astyle, freeimage) that don't set CC/CXX call
# them and fail unless this dir is on the build PATH. It is part of the gcc
# package, so it is present inside haikuporter's chroot via packagefs; the
# haikuporter wrapper below appends it to PATH so it reaches the in-chroot build.
CROSS_TOOLS_BIN="/boot/system/develop/tools/bin"
# The DeBeOS chain-planning tools (build-order graph + pool closure). Their source
# is deployed to $HP_HOME by haiku-bake-builder, alongside haiku-nativebuild; this
# script only installs the on-PATH launcher wrappers below. Override for a custom
# layout.
DEPCLOSURE_SRC="${DEPCLOSURE_SRC:-$HP_HOME/depclosure.py}"
PKGCLOSURE_SRC="${PKGCLOSURE_SRC:-$HP_HOME/haiku-package-closure}"

log() { echo "provision: $*"; }

# Feed pkgman non-interactively. The lean base ships bootstrap "Haiku Project"
# libs (e.g. zlib-1.2.13_bootstrap) that the DeBeOS repo supersedes with newer
# versions -- so an install that pulls a DeBeOS package needing the newer lib
# (curl -> lib:libz>=1.3.2) prompts to "allow vendor change" (solution 1). That
# numbered problem-resolution prompt is interactive even under `pkgman -y`, so
# without answering it the install aborts with "none of the providers can be
# installed". Feed a run of "1"s to accept each vendor change, then "yes" to
# confirm the transaction -- the same shape haiku-nativebuild uses.
feed() { i=0; while [ $i -lt 80 ]; do echo 1; i=$((i+1)); done; echo yes; }

# --- 1. toolchain from the repo -------------------------------------------
log "refreshing the package repo"
feed | pkgman refresh >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

# Kept as one install so the solver resolves the set together. Anything already
# active is a no-op -- which is NOT the same as "ends up at the pool's version":
# an active `*_bootstrap` package provides its own unversioned name, so this
# install leaves it in place. See the `pkgman update` loop after the install for
# the names where that distinction matters (issue 748). This is the set proven
# necessary to build real ports; add more build-requires here as new ports
# surface them.
#
# gettext (the TOOLS package, distinct from the gettext_libintl runtime lib) is
# what carries `cmd:autopoint` plus the autoconf/automake macros AM_GNU_GETTEXT*
# and AC_LIB_* (installed as /boot/system/data/gettext/m4/*.m4). Without it,
# `autoreconf` on any port that calls AM_GNU_GETTEXT fails -- ~11 ports in
# campaign #136 (issue #173) died at bootstrap for exactly this. The lean base
# ships only gettext_libintl; add the full gettext package so autopoint is on
# PATH and the m4 macros resolve.
#
# tar/unzip/which/dos2unix are on the HOST for a different reason than the chroot
# tools above: haikuporter runs a recipe's PATCH() (and downloadSource/unpackSource)
# on the builder HOST, before it sets up the per-port build chroot -- Main.py calls
# port.patchSource() ahead of port.build() (which is what enters ChrootSetup). So a
# PATCH() that shells out to one of these dies "command not found" on the host even
# when the recipe correctly declares it in BUILD_PREREQUIRES (that only reaches the
# chroot BUILD/INSTALL phases). hexcompare is the case: its recipe already declares
# cmd:dos2unix, yet its PATCH() (dos2unix general.h ...) failed host-side because the
# lean base has no dos2unix. Install them here so the host PATCH/prep phase has the
# same universal utilities the chroot gets from scriptletPrerequirements (gzip is
# already present above). The in-chroot BUILD/INSTALL coverage for gzip/tar/unzip/
# which is the ShellScriptlets.py patch in section 2a-3.
#
# coreutils is here for `uname`, not for the file utilities (issue 701). The image's
# coreutils is `coreutils-9.9_bootstrap-1-arm64`, whose patched `src/uname.c` switches
# on get_cpu_topology_info() over an x86-only case list and answers `other` for
# B_CPU_ARM_64. `uname -m` is unaffected -- that one is libroot's and correctly says
# `arm64` -- but CMakeDetermineSystem.cmake reaches `uname -m` only through its
# Linux|CYGWIN|MSYS|GNU|Android, Darwin and OpenBSD branches, so Haiku lands in the
# final else(), which runs `uname -p` and retries with `-m` ONLY if `-p` exits
# non-zero. `-p` exits zero printing `other`, so CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR becomes
# `other` for every cmake port built on an unprovisioned builder, and any port that
# selects NEON by matching that string silently loses it. Measured on
# i-01886408618b237de with three `uname` binaries and one `project(p701 NONE)`:
#   image coreutils 9.9_bootstrap  -> uname -p = other   CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR=other
#   pool  coreutils 9.11-2         -> uname -p = arm64   CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR=arm64
#   native-built coreutils 9.12-1  -> uname -p = arm64   CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR=arm64
# So the published pool already answers correctly and no rebuild is needed; the gap
# was only that provisioning never pulled it in over the bootstrap package.
TOOLS="gcc binutils make m4 patch gawk diffutils bison flex \
	python3.14 autoconf automake libtool perl gettext gettext_libintl \
	coreutils haiku_devel curl git gzip tar unzip which dos2unix xz_utils libxml2"
log "installing toolchain: $TOOLS"
feed | pkgman install $TOOLS

# `pkgman install coreutils` above CANNOT pull the pool's coreutils over the
# image's (issue 748). The bootstrap package is itself named `coreutils` and
# `provides: coreutils = 9.9 (compatible >= 8)`, so the solver sees the
# requirement already satisfied by the active package and answers
# "coreutils-9.9_bootstrap-1 from repository system is already installed.
# Nothing to do." The versioned name is not installable either -- `pkgman
# install coreutils-9.11-2` fails "Name not found", because that is a version,
# not a resolvable. The one verb that supersedes an active package with a newer
# one from a repository is `update`.
#
# This is the whole class, not just coreutils: every `*_bootstrap` package in
# the lean base provides its unversioned name, so `install` is a no-op against
# any of them. coreutils is the one that is load-bearing today (see the comment
# above section 1 for why `uname -p` matters), so it is the one handled here;
# add to the list if another bootstrap package turns out to matter.
for boot_pkg in coreutils; do
	log "superseding the bootstrap $boot_pkg with the pool's (issue 748)"
	feed | pkgman update "$boot_pkg" || log "WARNING: pkgman update $boot_pkg failed"
done

# Assert the outcome rather than the command's exit status. The reason 748
# existed at all is that the provisioner could not notice its own remedy had
# done nothing: there was no check, so every builder since has been provisioned
# with `uname -p` answering `other`. A builder that silently compiles ports for
# the wrong word size and byte order (issue 701) is worse than one that refuses
# to provision, so this is fatal.
uname_p="$(uname -p 2>/dev/null || true)"
case "$uname_p" in
arm64|aarch64)
	log "uname -p: $uname_p (ok)"
	;;
*)
	echo "provision: FATAL: uname -p answers '$uname_p', not arm64 -- the" \
		"image's coreutils is still active. Every cmake port built here" \
		"would get CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR=$uname_p, and tecmake-based" \
		"ports would compile -DTEC_BIGENDIAN -DTEC_32 (issues 701, 748)." \
		"Check that the DeBeOS repo is reachable (pkgman refresh) and that" \
		"it publishes a non-bootstrap coreutils, then re-run." >&2
	exit 1
	;;
esac

# python-zlib is the historically fatal gap -- fail loudly and early, not deep
# inside a tarfile stack trace, if this python cannot inflate a .tar.gz.
if ! python3.14 -c 'import zlib' >/dev/null 2>&1; then
	echo "provision: FATAL: python3.14 has no zlib module -- cannot unpack" \
		".tar.gz sources. Install a python build with zlib." >&2
	exit 1
fi
log "python3.14 zlib: ok"

# --- 1a. #136 Class 24: source-acquisition clients on the HOST ----------------
# A recipe whose SOURCE_URI uses a VCS or archive format (hg+/svn+/bzr+/.lha)
# needs that client to FETCH/UNPACK the source. Like the Class-2 host case
# (dos2unix), this runs on the builder HOST before the per-port chroot exists --
# haikuporter calls downloadSource/unpackSource (and patchSource) ahead of
# build()/ChrootSetup -- so a `cmd:` BUILD_PREREQUIRES cannot help; the client
# must be installed on the host. Without it the fetch dies before any compile
# with `Error: '<hg|svn|bzr|lha>' is not available, please install it`
# (cube2tesseract, ira/lha, previous, xemacs/hg -- issue #136 Class 24).
#
# Installed TOLERANTLY, one at a time: unlike the section-1 batch (which must
# resolve as a set or the whole install aborts), a source-acq client the arm64
# pool has not published yet must NOT break provisioning of an otherwise-working
# builder -- that port simply stays blocked on the unpublished client (a Class-20
# publish task), exactly as before this section existed. Idempotent: an
# already-active package is a no-op. Package names: hg=mercurial, svn=subversion,
# bzr=breezy, plus lha and cvs.
log "#136 Class 24: installing source-acquisition clients (tolerant): mercurial subversion breezy lha cvs"
for acq in mercurial subversion breezy lha cvs; do
	if feed | pkgman install "$acq" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
		log "#136 Class 24: $acq installed (or already present)"
	else
		log "#136 Class 24: $acq NOT available in the pool (skipped; a port needing"\
			"it stays a Class-20 publish task -- not fatal to provisioning)"
	fi
done

# haikuporter and many recipes run under `env python3` / `#!/usr/bin/env python3`
# shebangs, but the base userland ships only `python3.14`. Provide the unversioned
# name in the on-PATH shim dir so those invocations resolve.
mkdir -p "$SHIM_DIR"
ln -sf "$(command -v python3.14)" "$SHIM_DIR/python3"
log "python3 -> python3.14 symlink: $SHIM_DIR/python3"

# --- 1b. #171 stopgap: overlay the C89-safe gnu/ compat headers -------------
# The compatibility shims gnu/sched.h and gnu/crypt.h declare their static helper
# functions (__cpu_count, crypt_r) with the bare C99 keyword `inline`. A port
# compiled in strict C89/-ansi mode that pulls them in under _DEFAULT_SOURCE (via
# gnu/pthread.h -> sched.h) fails to parse:
#     gnu/sched.h:45:14: error: expected ';' before 'unsigned'
# -- which broke the argon2 and opusfile builds in campaign #136. The real fix
# (graviton cc9e418fbf / #171) is a one-token change, `inline` -> `__inline` (a
# GCC keyword accepted in every -std mode, matching the sibling bsd/ headers).
#
# But these are Haiku SYSTEM headers: they ship in the haiku_devel package baked
# into the canonical AMI, NOT in a spot the graviton source tree reaches at
# provision time. And haikuporter builds each port in a chroot whose /boot/system
# is a FRESH packagefs mounted from the activated hpkg FILES -- so what a build
# actually compiles against is the copy of the header INSIDE haiku_devel's hpkg,
# not any loose file on the host. So overlay the fix where it is consumed: patch
# the two headers inside the haiku_devel hpkg itself (package add -f), then let
# the AMI snapshot carry the patched file. Every builder launched from the AMI
# boots fresh, activates the patched haiku_devel, and its build chroots serve the
# fixed headers -- argon2/opusfile then build.
#
# STOPGAP: the clean long-term path is #171 flowing into the next canonical AMI
# bake, after which the headers are already __inline and this is a no-op. The
# substitution below reproduces cc9e418fbf byte-for-byte (`static inline` is the
# only match in either header), so it can never diverge from a bundled copy.
# Idempotent: skipped when the headers are already C89-safe.
overlay_gnu_compat_headers() {
	hd=$(ls /boot/system/packages/haiku_devel-*.hpkg 2>/dev/null | head -1)
	sh=/boot/system/develop/headers/gnu/sched.h
	ch=/boot/system/develop/headers/gnu/crypt.h
	if [ -z "$hd" ]; then
		echo "provision: FATAL #171 overlay: no haiku_devel hpkg in" \
			"/boot/system/packages" >&2
		exit 1
	fi
	if ! grep -q 'static inline' "$sh" 2>/dev/null \
			&& ! grep -q 'static inline' "$ch" 2>/dev/null; then
		log "#171 overlay: gnu/sched.h+crypt.h already C89-safe; skipping"
		return 0
	fi
	log "#171 overlay: patching gnu/sched.h+crypt.h inside $(basename "$hd")"
	rm -rf /tmp/gnu-overlay /tmp/gnu-overlay.hpkg /tmp/gnu-overlay-verify
	mkdir -p /tmp/gnu-overlay/develop/headers/gnu
	sed 's/static inline/static __inline/g' "$sh" \
		> /tmp/gnu-overlay/develop/headers/gnu/sched.h
	sed 's/static inline/static __inline/g' "$ch" \
		> /tmp/gnu-overlay/develop/headers/gnu/crypt.h
	cp "$hd" /tmp/gnu-overlay.hpkg
	# </dev/null on every `package` call: this runs under SSM with no TTY, and a
	# `package` sub-command that hits a prompt (e.g. extract into a missing dir)
	# otherwise loops on the inherited stdin instead of failing fast.
	package add -f -C /tmp/gnu-overlay /tmp/gnu-overlay.hpkg \
		develop/headers/gnu/sched.h develop/headers/gnu/crypt.h </dev/null
	# Verify the patched entry made it into the repacked hpkg BEFORE swapping --
	# never ship an overlay that silently no-op'd (success != artifact). Note:
	# `package extract -C DIR` chdir's into DIR and fails outright if it does not
	# already exist, so create it first.
	mkdir -p /tmp/gnu-overlay-verify
	package extract -C /tmp/gnu-overlay-verify /tmp/gnu-overlay.hpkg \
		develop/headers/gnu/sched.h </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
	if ! grep -q 'static __inline' \
			/tmp/gnu-overlay-verify/develop/headers/gnu/sched.h 2>/dev/null; then
		echo "provision: FATAL #171 overlay: patched header absent from" \
			"repacked haiku_devel hpkg" >&2
		exit 1
	fi
	# Atomic rename over the active file (the running host packagefs keeps the old
	# mmap'd inode; a fresh open -- including haikuporter's chroot mount -- reads
	# the new one). sync so the on-disk file, hence the --no-reboot AMI snapshot,
	# carries the patched package (BFS defers data flushes -- see the page-writer
	# note in haiku-ec2-no-sshd-after-stop-start).
	mv /tmp/gnu-overlay.hpkg "$hd"
	sync
	rm -rf /tmp/gnu-overlay /tmp/gnu-overlay-verify
	log "#171 overlay: haiku_devel patched + synced ($hd)"
}
overlay_gnu_compat_headers

# The canonical AMI's haiku_devel hpkg can lag the OS binary it ships beside: its
# published development headers were built before #329 (arm64: advertise NEON
# matrix/int8/bf16 caps in AT_HWCAP2) even though the kernel that advertises those
# AT_HWCAP2 bits at runtime is already in the image. A port that runtime-dispatches
# on HWCAP2_I8MM / HWCAP2_BF16 then fails to COMPILE -- the macros are absent from
# <sys/auxv.h> -- and has to fall back to baseline NEON, which is exactly the
# capability a rebased builder is meant to unblock. Same consumption path as the
# #171 overlay above: haikuporter builds in a chroot served from the haiku_devel
# hpkg FILES, so the CURRENT public header (staged from the DeBeOS source tree by
# haiku-bake-builder into AUXV_OVERLAY_S3) is repacked INTO that hpkg.
#
# Unlike #171 the corrected content cannot be sed-derived from the stale header, so
# it is fetched whole; the fetched copy is asserted to carry the fix before it is
# repacked, and the repacked entry is re-extracted and re-checked before the swap
# (success != artifact). STOPGAP, self-retiring: skipped when the installed header
# already defines HWCAP2_I8MM -- i.e. once a haiku_devel built from #329 source
# ships in the repo, or AUXV_OVERLAY_S3 is unset.
AUXV_OVERLAY_S3="${AUXV_OVERLAY_S3:-}"
# A presigned GET for the same object (issue 728). This script runs ON the box,
# which since the #302 agent migration has no S3 client at all -- the real
# amazon-ssm-agent has no `s3` verbs and the interim debeos-ssm-agent is gone --
# so it cannot resolve an s3:// URI itself. haiku-bake-builder presigns where the
# credentials are and passes the URL in. Preferred when set; AUXV_OVERLAY_S3 is
# still honoured for a box that does have a client.
AUXV_OVERLAY_URL="${AUXV_OVERLAY_URL:-}"
overlay_public_auxv_header() {
	if [ -z "$AUXV_OVERLAY_URL" ] && [ -z "$AUXV_OVERLAY_S3" ]; then
		log "#329 overlay: neither AUXV_OVERLAY_URL nor AUXV_OVERLAY_S3 set; skipping"
		return 0
	fi
	dst=/boot/system/develop/headers/posix/sys/auxv.h
	if grep -q 'HWCAP2_I8MM' "$dst" 2>/dev/null; then
		log "#329 overlay: <sys/auxv.h> already defines HWCAP2_I8MM; skipping"
		return 0
	fi
	hd=$(ls /boot/system/packages/haiku_devel-*.hpkg 2>/dev/null | head -1)
	if [ -z "$hd" ]; then
		echo "provision: FATAL #329 overlay: no haiku_devel hpkg in" \
			"/boot/system/packages" >&2
		exit 1
	fi
	rm -rf /tmp/auxv-overlay /tmp/auxv-overlay.hpkg /tmp/auxv-overlay-verify
	mkdir -p /tmp/auxv-overlay/develop/headers/posix/sys
	_auxv_dst=/tmp/auxv-overlay/develop/headers/posix/sys/auxv.h
	if [ -n "$AUXV_OVERLAY_URL" ]; then
		# Section 1 above installed curl, so it is on PATH by the time this runs.
		log "#329 overlay: fetching current <sys/auxv.h> over a presigned URL"
		if ! curl -fsSL --retry 3 --retry-delay 5 -o "$_auxv_dst" \
				"$AUXV_OVERLAY_URL"; then
			echo "provision: FATAL #329 overlay: could not fetch the presigned" \
				"auxv.h URL (expired? see issue 728)" >&2
			exit 1
		fi
	else
		log "#329 overlay: fetching current <sys/auxv.h> from $AUXV_OVERLAY_S3"
		if ! /boot/system/bin/debeos-ssm-agent s3 cp "$AUXV_OVERLAY_S3" \
				"$_auxv_dst" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
			echo "provision: FATAL #329 overlay: could not fetch" \
				"$AUXV_OVERLAY_S3. This image has no S3 client (issue 728):" \
				"pass AUXV_OVERLAY_URL instead -- haiku-bake-builder does." >&2
			exit 1
		fi
	fi
	if ! grep -q 'HWCAP2_I8MM' \
			/tmp/auxv-overlay/develop/headers/posix/sys/auxv.h 2>/dev/null; then
		echo "provision: FATAL #329 overlay: staged auxv.h lacks HWCAP2_I8MM --" \
			"refusing to repack a header that would not deliver the fix" >&2
		exit 1
	fi
	log "#329 overlay: repacking <sys/auxv.h> into $(basename "$hd")"
	cp "$hd" /tmp/auxv-overlay.hpkg
	# </dev/null on every `package` call for the same no-TTY reason as #171 above.
	package add -f -C /tmp/auxv-overlay /tmp/auxv-overlay.hpkg \
		develop/headers/posix/sys/auxv.h </dev/null
	mkdir -p /tmp/auxv-overlay-verify
	package extract -C /tmp/auxv-overlay-verify /tmp/auxv-overlay.hpkg \
		develop/headers/posix/sys/auxv.h </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
	if ! grep -q 'HWCAP2_I8MM' \
			/tmp/auxv-overlay-verify/develop/headers/posix/sys/auxv.h 2>/dev/null; then
		echo "provision: FATAL #329 overlay: patched header absent from" \
			"repacked haiku_devel hpkg" >&2
		exit 1
	fi
	# Atomic swap + sync, same rationale as #171: the running host keeps the old
	# mmap'd inode, but a fresh boot (and haikuporter's chroot mount) serves the new
	# header, and the sync makes the --no-reboot AMI snapshot carry it.
	mv /tmp/auxv-overlay.hpkg "$hd"
	sync
	rm -rf /tmp/auxv-overlay /tmp/auxv-overlay-verify
	log "#329 overlay: haiku_devel patched + synced ($hd)"
}
overlay_public_auxv_header

# --- 2. ports tree + haikuporter + config ---------------------------------
if [ ! -d "$PORTS_DIR/.git" ]; then
	log "cloning ports tree (shallow) -> $PORTS_DIR"
	git clone --depth 1 "$PORTS_REPO" "$PORTS_DIR"
else
	log "ports tree already present: $PORTS_DIR"
fi

# --- 2b. the DeBeOS recipe overlay (issue 764) -----------------------------
#
# The clone above is PRISTINE upstream HaikuPorts, which knows nothing about
# arm64 Haiku. Every recipe fix this project has merged lives in
# graviton/haikuports-patches/recipes/ and reaches a ports tree only through
# haiku-ports-overlay-apply -- and until this section existed, the ONLY caller of
# that script was haiku-bake-builder. So the overlay was applied while baking a
# builder AMI and never when provisioning an instance directly, which is the
# path graviton/docs/native-ec2-builds.md documents for building ports on a
# canonical-AMI box. A builder provisioned that way silently carried none of the
# project's recipe fixes and re-failed ports the tracker says are fixed.
#
# Measured, not inferred: instance i-092201b33570a801d, provisioned 2026-10-16
# from this script at d0eacc5aed, had the pristine upstream
# app-text/tesseract/tesseract-3.05.02.recipe with `autogen.sh` at line 86 --
# the exact bug commit 562c42b98b fixed and checked in under recipes/. That
# killed the gri -> texlive_core -> ghostscript_gpl -> tesseract chain at the
# bottom for an already-solved defect.
#
# Obtaining the overlay: OVERLAY_DIR if it is already on the box (what the bake
# stages, so a baked builder re-provisioning uses its own copies), otherwise a
# sparse shallow clone of the DeBeOS repo -- public, so no credentials, and
# --filter=blob:none --sparse keeps it to the two paths needed rather than the
# whole Haiku tree, which matters on a 20 GiB BFS root. The scratch clone is
# removed once the two paths are copied out.
#
# NOT skipped on failure. A fix-less builder is indistinguishable from a correct
# one until a port fails with an error the tracker calls closed, so this is
# FATAL -- the same contract as the #171/#329/#758 patchers, which abort rather
# than install a silent no-op. OVERLAY_SKIP=1 is the explicit, logged opt-out.
#
# --stage1 is deliberately NOT passed: a native builder's output goes to the
# shipping DeBeOS repo, and the overlay's stage-1 recipes are feature-capped
# bootstrap expedients ("never ship feature-capped builds", AGENTS.md).
fetch_recipe_overlay() {
	if [ -d "$OVERLAY_DIR/recipes" ] && [ -f "$OVERLAY_APPLY" ]; then
		log "#764 overlay: using $OVERLAY_DIR (already on this box)"
		return 0
	fi
	log "#764 overlay: fetching from $OVERLAY_REPO ($OVERLAY_REF)"
	_src="$HP_HOME/.overlay-src"
	rm -rf "$_src"
	# A git too old for partial clone falls back to a full depth-1 clone on its
	# own and still produces the paths, so this is a size optimisation, not a
	# correctness dependency.
	if ! git clone --depth 1 --branch "$OVERLAY_REF" --filter=blob:none \
			--sparse "$OVERLAY_REPO" "$_src"; then
		echo "provision: FATAL #764 overlay: cannot clone $OVERLAY_REPO" \
			"($OVERLAY_REF). Set OVERLAY_DIR to a local copy of" \
			"graviton/haikuports-patches, or OVERLAY_SKIP=1 to provision a" \
			"builder that will re-fail already-fixed ports." >&2
		exit 1
	fi
	(cd "$_src" && git sparse-checkout set graviton/haikuports-patches \
		graviton/scripts >/dev/null)
	if [ ! -d "$_src/graviton/haikuports-patches/recipes" ] \
			|| [ ! -f "$_src/graviton/scripts/haiku-ports-overlay-apply" ]; then
		echo "provision: FATAL #764 overlay: the sparse checkout of" \
			"$OVERLAY_REPO ($OVERLAY_REF) has no" \
			"graviton/haikuports-patches/recipes or no" \
			"haiku-ports-overlay-apply -- the repo layout moved" >&2
		exit 1
	fi
	rm -rf "$OVERLAY_DIR"
	cp -r "$_src/graviton/haikuports-patches" "$OVERLAY_DIR"
	cp "$_src/graviton/scripts/haiku-ports-overlay-apply" "$OVERLAY_APPLY"
	chmod +x "$OVERLAY_APPLY"
	rm -rf "$_src"
	log "#764 overlay: fetched -> $OVERLAY_DIR"
}

apply_recipe_overlay() {
	if [ "$OVERLAY_SKIP" = 1 ]; then
		echo "provision: WARNING #764 overlay: OVERLAY_SKIP=1 -- the ports tree" \
			"is pristine upstream and carries NONE of this project's arm64" \
			"recipe fixes. Ports the tracker calls fixed will fail again." >&2
		return 0
	fi
	fetch_recipe_overlay
	log "#764 overlay: applying $OVERLAY_DIR -> $PORTS_DIR (no --stage1)"
	if ! /bin/sh "$OVERLAY_APPLY" "$OVERLAY_DIR" "$PORTS_DIR"; then
		echo "provision: FATAL #764 overlay: haiku-ports-overlay-apply failed." \
			"It exits non-zero when a recipe cannot be placed in the tree, which" \
			"means that fix would be silently unused -- see its WARNING lines" \
			"above for which ones." >&2
		exit 1
	fi
	# Success is not an artifact. Count the overlay recipes that are now
	# byte-identical in the tree, and refuse a run where the overlay reached
	# nothing -- a summary line of "0 updated, 0 placed" is exactly the silent
	# no-op this section exists to end.
	_landed=0
	for _r in "$OVERLAY_DIR"/recipes/*.recipe; do
		[ -f "$_r" ] || continue
		_b=$(basename "$_r")
		_t=$(find "$PORTS_DIR" -name "$_b" -not -path "*/work-*" 2>/dev/null | head -1)
		[ -n "$_t" ] || continue
		cmp -s "$_r" "$_t" && _landed=$((_landed + 1))
	done
	if [ "$_landed" -eq 0 ]; then
		echo "provision: FATAL #764 overlay: haiku-ports-overlay-apply reported" \
			"success but not one overlay recipe is byte-identical in" \
			"$PORTS_DIR -- the overlay did not reach the tree" >&2
		exit 1
	fi
	log "#764 overlay: $_landed overlay recipe(s) verified byte-identical in $PORTS_DIR"
}
apply_recipe_overlay

if [ ! -d "$HP_DIR/.git" ] && [ ! -x "$HP_DIR/haikuporter" ]; then
	log "cloning haikuporter -> $HP_DIR"
	git clone --depth 1 "$HP_REPO" "$HP_DIR"
else
	log "haikuporter already present: $HP_DIR"
fi

# --- 2a. #47: default CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM for every CMake port --------
# cmake 4.x dropped the compatibility shim for cmake_minimum_required(<3.5), so a
# port -- or a bundled third-party tree inside one -- that declares an old minimum
# fails at configure. Rather than add -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 to each
# recipe (openal, libjxl, json_c did so one-off), append it once to haikuporter's
# shared cmakeDirArgs shell variable, which every CMake recipe already passes to
# `cmake`. It is a cache variable, so it reaches add_subdirectory()'d bundles
# (libjxl's sjpeg/highway) too, and it only raises the policy floor -- projects
# asking for a newer minimum are unaffected.
#
# LIMIT of the cmakeDirArgs channel (why 2a-2 below also exists, #136): it only
# reaches recipes that actually PASS $cmakeDirArgs to cmake. Most CMake recipes
# hand-write an explicit `cmake -D... <src>` argument list and never reference
# $cmakeDirArgs, so the flag never gets to them -- which is why dozens of #136
# ports still had to be fixed one recipe at a time. The channel that reaches
# EVERY cmake invocation regardless of how the recipe spells it is the build
# environment: cmake >= 3.31 reads CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM from os.environ.
# Section 2a-2 injects it there. Both are kept: 2a-2 is the systemic reach, 2a
# stays as belt-and-braces for a builder whose cmake predates 3.31 (the env var
# is then ignored and the -D on $cmakeDirArgs-using recipes still helps).
# Anchored, idempotent, and verified (a haikuporter that moved the anchor fails
# loudly rather than installing a silent no-op). See
# graviton/haikuports-patches/haikuporter-cmake-policy-minimum.patch (#47/#136).
log "#47: defaulting CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 in haikuporter cmakeDirArgs"
python3.14 - "$HP_DIR/HaikuPorter/Port.py" <<'PY'
import io, py_compile, sys
path = sys.argv[1]
with io.open(path, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
    text = fh.read()
if 'CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM' in text:
    print("provision: #47 cmakeDirArgs already patched; skipping")
    sys.exit(0)
anchor = "for k, v in cmakeDirArgs.items())\n"
count = text.count(anchor)
if count != 1:
    sys.stderr.write(
        "provision: FATAL #47: cmakeDirArgs anchor appears %d times in %s, "
        "expected once -- haikuporter changed, re-check the patch\n"
        % (count, path))
    sys.exit(1)
addition = (
    "for k, v in cmakeDirArgs.items())\n"
    "\t\t# graviton/DeBeOS (#47): default the floor policy so cmake 4.x still\n"
    "\t\t# configures projects/bundled trees declaring cmake_minimum_required\n"
    "\t\t# (<3.5). Cache var -> reaches add_subdirectory() bundles; projects\n"
    "\t\t# asking for a newer minimum are unaffected.\n"
    "\t\tself.shellVariables['cmakeDirArgs'] += "
    "' -DCMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5'\n"
)
with io.open(path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
    fh.write(text.replace(anchor, addition, 1))
try:
    py_compile.compile(path, cfile='/tmp/.hp47-check.pyc', doraise=True)
except Exception as exc:
    sys.stderr.write("provision: FATAL #47: patched Port.py does not compile: "
        "%s: %s\n" % (type(exc).__name__, exc))
    sys.exit(1)
print("provision: #47 cmakeDirArgs patched")
PY

# --- 2a-2. #136: make CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM reach EVERY cmake, via env --
# The 2a cmakeDirArgs edit only helps recipes that pass $cmakeDirArgs to cmake.
# Most CMake recipes write an explicit `cmake -D... <src>` arg list and never
# reference it, so 2a misses them -- that is why the #136 waves still fixed the
# cmake_minimum_required(<3.5) failure one recipe at a time (json_c, openal,
# libjxl, tweeny, and dozens more). cmake >= 3.31 reads the cache variable
# CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM from the ENVIRONMENT, applied before it parses any
# CMakeLists.txt, so a value in the build shell's environment reaches every cmake
# invocation regardless of how the recipe calls it. haikuporter builds each port
# in a shell whose environment is exactly what HaikuPorter/Utils.py:
# filteredEnvironment() returns -- it deliberately drops the host environment
# (LC_ALL/TERM defaults + LIBRARY_PATH/PATH passed through) so one port's exports
# cannot leak into another. So the durable place to set this default is inside
# filteredEnvironment() itself: seed CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 into the
# returned dict (next to the LC_ALL/TERM defaults) and pass any explicit
# os.environ override through, so a recipe/operator that sets a different value
# still wins. Only raises the policy floor; projects requesting a newer minimum
# are unaffected. Anchored on the passthrough loop, idempotent, byte-compiled to
# verify -- a moved anchor fails loudly. See
# graviton/haikuports-patches/haikuporter-cmake-policy-minimum.patch (#136).
log "#136: seeding CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM=3.5 in haikuporter filteredEnvironment"
python3.14 - "$HP_DIR/HaikuPorter/Utils.py" <<'PY'
import io, py_compile, sys
path = sys.argv[1]
with io.open(path, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
    text = fh.read()
if 'CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM' in text:
    print("provision: #136 filteredEnvironment already patched; skipping")
    sys.exit(0)
# Anchor on the exact env-dict default block and the passthrough loop that
# follow it in filteredEnvironment(). Both are matched so a haikuporter refactor
# of either one fails loudly instead of silently no-op'ing.
env_anchor = (
    "\tenv = {\n"
    "\t\t'LC_ALL': 'POSIX',\n"
    "\t\t'TERM': 'xterm',\n"
    "\t}\n"
)
loop_anchor = "\tfor key in ['LIBRARY_PATH', 'PATH']:\n"
for name, anchor in (('env-dict', env_anchor), ('passthrough-loop', loop_anchor)):
    c = text.count(anchor)
    if c != 1:
        sys.stderr.write(
            "provision: FATAL #136: filteredEnvironment %s anchor appears %d "
            "times in %s, expected once -- haikuporter changed, re-check the "
            "patch\n" % (name, c, path))
        sys.exit(1)
new_env = (
    "\tenv = {\n"
    "\t\t'LC_ALL': 'POSIX',\n"
    "\t\t'TERM': 'xterm',\n"
    "\t\t# graviton/DeBeOS (#136): default cmake's policy floor to 3.5 for EVERY\n"
    "\t\t# cmake invocation in a port BUILD(), independent of how the recipe\n"
    "\t\t# calls cmake. cmake >= 3.31 reads CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM from the\n"
    "\t\t# environment, so this reaches recipes that pass an explicit arg list\n"
    "\t\t# and never reference $cmakeDirArgs. Only raises the floor; an explicit\n"
    "\t\t# override in os.environ still wins (passthrough loop below).\n"
    "\t\t'CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM': '3.5',\n"
    "\t}\n"
)
new_loop = "\tfor key in ['LIBRARY_PATH', 'PATH', 'CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM']:\n"
text = text.replace(env_anchor, new_env, 1).replace(loop_anchor, new_loop, 1)
with io.open(path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
    fh.write(text)
try:
    py_compile.compile(path, cfile='/tmp/.hp136-check.pyc', doraise=True)
except Exception as exc:
    sys.stderr.write("provision: FATAL #136: patched Utils.py does not compile: "
        "%s: %s\n" % (type(exc).__name__, exc))
    sys.exit(1)
print("provision: #136 filteredEnvironment patched")
PY

# --- 2a-3. #136 Class 2: seed universal build tools into EVERY chroot ----------
# The #27/Class-2 failure: a port shells out to a common utility (gzip, tar,
# unzip, which) during BUILD()/INSTALL() -- almost always compressing a man page
# on `make install`, unpacking a bundled archive, or a configure/autogen.sh probe
# -- but never declares it in BUILD_PREREQUIRES. haikuporter mounts ONLY a
# recipe's declared prerequisites (plus a fixed base set) into the build chroot,
# so the tool is absent even though the builder HOST has it installed, and the
# build dies `command not found` / `Error 127`. It was the single largest
# auto-recoverable non-CMake class in the #136 backlog (gzip alone recurred
# across atari++, convmv, djvu, kakoune, libtermkey, mdate, unibilium).
#
# Fixing this one recipe at a time (a `cmd:gzip` per recipe overlay) is
# whack-a-mole AND only reaches ports we have already touched -- and, because a
# recipe overlay is baked into the AMI's ports tree, it needs a full rebake to
# take effect anyway. The systemic lever is haikuporter's OWN base prerequisite
# set: HaikuPorter/ShellScriptlets.py:scriptletPrerequirements is the list of
# commands haikuporter activates into every chroot regardless of the recipe (it
# already carries coreutils, cmd:bash, cmd:find, cmd:grep, cmd:sed, the
# objcopy/readelf/strip triplet, cmd:xres). Appending the four universal build
# utilities there puts them in EVERY build's chroot at once -- exactly the
# mechanism that already supplies sed/grep/coreutils, all of which are themselves
# ports, so this adds no new self-dependency class. Build-time only: scriptlet
# prerequisites never become the built package's runtime REQUIRES.
#
# Scope is deliberately the four utilities any build/install step reasonably
# assumes are present, the same "universal" bar the existing entries meet:
#   cmd:gzip  cmd:tar  cmd:unzip  cmd:which
# Feature-specific tools stay per-recipe: gettext's msgfmt/msgattrib (an i18n
# port only), dos2unix (a patch-time fixup) -- putting the 16 MB gettext package
# in every chroot would over-broaden.
#
# LOAD-BEARING COUPLING with the TOOLS install in section 1: haikuporter resolves
# build prerequisites against the builder's LOCAL package pool only, never the
# remote repo -- so a scriptlet prerequisite that is not INSTALLED would be
# unresolvable and would break EVERY build. gzip/tar/unzip/which are therefore in
# the section-1 TOOLS `pkgman install` set; that is what makes these four entries
# resolvable in the chroot, not merely their presence in the remote pool (all four
# do exist there for arm64: app-arch/gzip, app-arch/tar, app-arch/unzip,
# sys-apps/which). Do not add a tool here without also installing it there.
# Anchored, idempotent, byte-compiled to verify -- a moved
# anchor fails loudly rather than installing a silent no-op. See
# graviton/haikuports-patches/haikuporter-scriptlet-build-tools.patch (#136).
log "#136 Class 2: adding cmd:gzip/tar/unzip/which to haikuporter scriptletPrerequirements"
python3.14 - "$HP_DIR/HaikuPorter/ShellScriptlets.py" <<'PY'
import io, py_compile, sys
path = sys.argv[1]
with io.open(path, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
    text = fh.read()
tools = ['cmd:gzip', 'cmd:tar', 'cmd:unzip', 'cmd:which']
if all(('\t' + t + '\n') in text for t in tools):
    print("provision: #136 scriptletPrerequirements already patched; skipping")
    sys.exit(0)
# Anchor on the tail of the base prerequisite block (the cmd:xres line that ends
# the list, followed by the closing triple-quote). Matched exactly once so a
# haikuporter change to the base set fails loudly instead of silently no-op'ing.
anchor = "\tcmd:xres\n'''\n"
count = text.count(anchor)
if count != 1:
    sys.stderr.write(
        "provision: FATAL #136 Class 2: scriptletPrerequirements anchor appears "
        "%d times in %s, expected once -- haikuporter changed, re-check the "
        "patch\n" % (count, path))
    sys.exit(1)
addition = (
    "\tcmd:xres\n"
    "\t# graviton/DeBeOS (#136, Class 2): universal build utilities every recipe\n"
    "\t# reasonably assumes are present -- man-page gzip on `make install`, a\n"
    "\t# bundled-archive tar/unzip, a which-based configure probe -- but that\n"
    "\t# recipes routinely forget to declare. Same 'universal tool' bar as the\n"
    "\t# entries above (all of which are themselves ports), so no new\n"
    "\t# self-dependency class. Build-time only. Feature-specific tools\n"
    "\t# (gettext msgfmt/msgattrib, dos2unix) stay per-recipe.\n"
    "\tcmd:gzip\n"
    "\tcmd:tar\n"
    "\tcmd:unzip\n"
    "\tcmd:which\n"
    "'''\n"
)
with io.open(path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
    fh.write(text.replace(anchor, addition, 1))
try:
    py_compile.compile(path, cfile='/tmp/.hp136c2-check.pyc', doraise=True)
except Exception as exc:
    sys.stderr.write("provision: FATAL #136 Class 2: patched ShellScriptlets.py "
        "does not compile: %s: %s\n" % (type(exc).__name__, exc))
    sys.exit(1)
print("provision: #136 scriptletPrerequirements patched")
PY

# --- 2a-4. #136 Class 8: default CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE in haikuporter's cmake wrapper -
# The Class-8 failure: a recipe calls `cmake` for its configure step without any
# -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE (8 of the 10 #136 Class-8 ports), or with RelWithDebInfo but
# no _debuginfo subpackage (2 of them). haikuporter shadows `cmake` in the BUILD()
# shell with a wrapper (HaikuPorter/ShellScriptlets.py:cmake()) that *aborts* in
# both cases -- "invoking cmake without CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE specified!" and "with
# -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo without debug info packages specified".
#
# WHY NOT the #47/#136 CMAKE_POLICY_VERSION_MINIMUM channels. Neither reaches this:
#   * The wrapper inspects the cmake COMMAND LINE ("$*") only, never the env, so a
#     value seeded in filteredEnvironment() (the #136 policy-floor channel) does
#     not satisfy it -- and cmake reads CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE only from -D/cache, never
#     the environment, so there is no env channel to use in the first place.
#   * Appending -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release to cmakeDirArgs (the #47 channel) would
#     be actively HARMFUL: a known-good library recipe that passes both
#     $cmakeDirArgs and its own -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo *with* a
#     _debuginfo subpackage would then match the wrapper's "Release WITH debug info
#     packages specified" guard and start aborting. It also only reaches recipes
#     that interpolate $cmakeDirArgs (most hand-write an explicit arg list).
#
# So the systemic lever is the wrapper itself. We change ONLY its two branches
# that already ended in `exit 1` -- i.e. the always-fail paths, which no
# known-good port ever traversed, so the no-regression surface is zero:
#   (A) no CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE at all      -> inject -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
#   (B) RelWithDebInfo, no _debuginfo   -> rewrite that token to Release
# Both are exactly the wrapper's own printed advice ("you probably want Release").
# The "Release WITH debug info packages" guard (elif #2) is left byte-for-byte
# intact, so a real Release/debuginfo mismatch still fails loudly. This reaches
# EVERY cmake port regardless of how it spells the invocation, and -- unlike a
# per-recipe overlay -- does not depend on the ISP-source-package overlay landing
# on the tree with a correctly-pinned mtime (the fragile path that let 4 already-
# overlaid Class-8 recipes regress in the wave). Anchored, idempotent, byte-
# compiled to verify. See
# graviton/haikuports-patches/haikuporter-cmake-build-type-default.patch (#136).
log "#136 Class 8: defaulting CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release in haikuporter cmake wrapper"
python3.14 - "$HP_DIR/HaikuPorter/ShellScriptlets.py" <<'PY'
import io, py_compile, sys
path = sys.argv[1]
with io.open(path, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
    text = fh.read()
marker = "graviton/DeBeOS (#136, Class 8)"
if marker in text:
    print("provision: #136 Class 8 cmake wrapper already patched; skipping")
    sys.exit(0)

# (A) absent CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE: default to Release instead of aborting.
anchorA = (
    "\t\tif [[ \"$*\" != *CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE* ]]; then\n"
    "\t\t\techo \"error: invoking cmake without CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE specified!\"\n"
    "\t\t\techo \"note: you probably want -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release or "
    "-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo\"\n"
    "\t\t\texit 1\n"
)
cA = text.count(anchorA)
if cA != 1:
    sys.stderr.write(
        "provision: FATAL #136 Class 8: cmake-wrapper anchor A appears %d times "
        "in %s, expected once -- haikuporter changed, re-check the patch\n"
        % (cA, path))
    sys.exit(1)
replA = (
    "\t\tif [[ \"$*\" != *CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE* ]]; then\n"
    "\t\t\t# graviton/DeBeOS (#136, Class 8): a recipe that calls cmake with no\n"
    "\t\t\t# CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE previously aborted here. Supply the wrapper's own\n"
    "\t\t\t# recommended default (Release) instead, so every such port configures\n"
    "\t\t\t# regardless of whether it uses $cmakeDirArgs or an explicit arg list.\n"
    "\t\t\t# cmake reads CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE only from -D/cache (not the env), so this\n"
    "\t\t\t# cannot be an env default; only this always-failed branch changes --\n"
    "\t\t\t# recipes that already pass a type are untouched.\n"
    "\t\t\tset -- -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \"$@\"\n"
)
text = text.replace(anchorA, replA, 1)

# (B) RelWithDebInfo without a _debuginfo subpackage: rewrite to Release.
anchorB = (
    "\t\telif [[ \"$*\" = *CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo* ]] && "
    "[ -z \"$DEBUG_INFO_PACKAGES\" ]; then\n"
    "\t\t\techo -n \"error: invoking cmake with -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo "
    "without debug info \"\n"
    "\t\t\techo \"packages specified\"\n"
    "\t\t\techo \"note: you probably want -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release\"\n"
    "\t\t\texit 1\n"
)
cB = text.count(anchorB)
if cB != 1:
    sys.stderr.write(
        "provision: FATAL #136 Class 8: cmake-wrapper anchor B appears %d times "
        "in %s, expected once -- haikuporter changed, re-check the patch\n"
        % (cB, path))
    sys.exit(1)
replB = (
    "\t\telif [[ \"$*\" = *CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo* ]] && "
    "[ -z \"$DEBUG_INFO_PACKAGES\" ]; then\n"
    "\t\t\t# graviton/DeBeOS (#136, Class 8): the recipe asked for RelWithDebInfo\n"
    "\t\t\t# but declared no _debuginfo subpackage, which previously aborted.\n"
    "\t\t\t# Rewrite the requested type to Release (the wrapper's own advice) so\n"
    "\t\t\t# the port builds; only this always-failed branch changes.\n"
    "\t\t\tset -- \"${@/CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo/CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release}\"\n"
)
text = text.replace(anchorB, replB, 1)

with io.open(path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
    fh.write(text)
try:
    py_compile.compile(path, cfile='/tmp/.hp136c8-check.pyc', doraise=True)
except Exception as exc:
    sys.stderr.write("provision: FATAL #136 Class 8: patched ShellScriptlets.py "
        "does not compile: %s: %s\n" % (type(exc).__name__, exc))
    sys.exit(1)
print("provision: #136 Class 8 cmake wrapper patched")
PY

# --- 2a-5. #758: batch packageEntries' per-file dirname/mkdir/mv ---------------
# haikuporter's packageEntries() (HaikuPorter/ShellScriptlets.py) is the shell
# helper every recipe's INSTALL() uses to move built files into a subpackage's
# staging directory. Upstream forks THREE processes per entry -- `dirname`,
# `mkdir -p`, `mv` -- which is invisible for the dozens of entries a normal
# recipe packages, and fatal for app-text/texlive, whose INSTALL() hands it
# ~400k entries. Measured on the native arm64 builder at ~27 entries/s, that is
# ~7 hours in a single phase; the run was SIGKILLed partway through with no
# output and no error, which reads as a build failure rather than as a loop that
# simply had not finished (issue 758).
#
# This is haikuporter's own code path, not a recipe's, so there is no per-recipe
# lever: a recipe cannot batch a function it only calls. Same channel as the
# #136/#47 patches above and for the same reason.
#
# Three changes, none of which alter what ends up where:
#   * `dirname` -> `${targetDir%/*}`. No fork. Safe because $packagePrefix is
#     absolute, so the string always contains a slash -- the one case where the
#     two differ is a doubled slash (`a//b`), where the parameter expansion
#     yields `$packagePrefix/a/` and `dirname` yields `$packagePrefix/a`; both
#     name the same directory, so the only effect is one extra mkdir.
#   * `mkdir -p` only when the target directory actually changes. Entry lists
#     reach here grouped by directory (they come from find/glob output), so in
#     practice this is one mkdir per directory.
#   * `mv` batched per directory, flushed when the directory changes and capped
#     at 256 entries -- ~31 KB of argv, far under ARG_MAX -- so a single flat
#     directory holding 400k files does not build one unbounded command line.
#
# Verified byte-equivalent to the upstream loop by running both over the SAME
# 648-entry fixture (deep nesting, a 600-entry flat directory, names with
# spaces, a literal `$`, an embedded newline, a bare directory entry, and an
# absolute-form entry) with counting shims on all three commands: identical
# package tree, identical leftovers under $installDestDir, identical
# stdout/stderr, 1944 forks -> 24. `graviton/scripts/haiku-packageentries-selftest`
# carries that harness. See
# graviton/haikuports-patches/haikuporter-packageentries-batch.patch (issue 758).
#
# `lastDir` and `batch` MUST be `local`: packageDebugInfos() calls
# packageEntries() again inside its own loop, so leaked state would flush a
# stale batch into the wrong directory. `targetDir` is deliberately left
# non-local, exactly as upstream leaks it.
log "#758: batching packageEntries' per-file dirname/mkdir/mv in haikuporter"
python3.14 - "$HP_DIR/HaikuPorter/ShellScriptlets.py" <<'PY'
import io, py_compile, sys
path = sys.argv[1]
with io.open(path, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
    text = fh.read()
marker = "graviton/DeBeOS (issue 758)"
if marker in text:
    print("provision: #758 packageEntries already patched; skipping")
    sys.exit(0)

# Anchor A: the head of the move loop, where the batch state is declared.
anchorA = "\t# move the entries provided\n\tfor file; do\n"
# Anchor B: the three forks themselves, plus the loop terminator so the flush
# can be appended after it.
anchorB = (
    "\t\t# make sure target containing directory exists and move there\n"
    "\t\ttargetDir=$(dirname \"$packagePrefix/$file\")\n"
    "\t\tmkdir -p \"$targetDir\"\n"
    "\t\tmv \"$installDestDir$prefix/$file\" \"$targetDir\"\n"
    "\tdone\n"
)
for name, anchor in (("A", anchorA), ("B", anchorB)):
    count = text.count(anchor)
    if count != 1:
        sys.stderr.write(
            "provision: FATAL #758: packageEntries anchor %s appears %d times "
            "in %s, expected once -- haikuporter changed, re-check the patch\n"
            % (name, count, path))
        sys.exit(1)

replA = (
    "\t# move the entries provided\n"
    "\t#\n"
    "\t# graviton/DeBeOS (issue 758): batched. Upstream forked dirname + mkdir\n"
    "\t# -p + mv per entry, which is ~7 hours for app-text/texlive's ~400k-entry\n"
    "\t# INSTALL(). lastDir/batch are local because packageDebugInfos() re-enters\n"
    "\t# this function.\n"
    "\tlocal lastDir=\n"
    "\tlocal -a batch=()\n"
    "\tfor file; do\n"
)
replB = (
    "\t\t# make sure target containing directory exists and move there,\n"
    "\t\t# batching the move per directory (issue 758)\n"
    "\t\ttargetDir=\"$packagePrefix/$file\"\n"
    "\t\ttargetDir=${targetDir%/*}\n"
    "\t\tif [ \"$targetDir\" != \"$lastDir\" ]; then\n"
    "\t\t\tif [ ${#batch[@]} -gt 0 ]; then\n"
    "\t\t\t\tmv \"${batch[@]}\" \"$lastDir\"\n"
    "\t\t\t\tbatch=()\n"
    "\t\t\tfi\n"
    "\t\t\tmkdir -p \"$targetDir\"\n"
    "\t\t\tlastDir=$targetDir\n"
    "\t\telif [ ${#batch[@]} -ge 256 ]; then\n"
    "\t\t\t# bound the argv a single flat directory can build\n"
    "\t\t\tmv \"${batch[@]}\" \"$lastDir\"\n"
    "\t\t\tbatch=()\n"
    "\t\tfi\n"
    "\t\tbatch+=(\"$installDestDir$prefix/$file\")\n"
    "\tdone\n"
    "\tif [ ${#batch[@]} -gt 0 ]; then\n"
    "\t\tmv \"${batch[@]}\" \"$lastDir\"\n"
    "\tfi\n"
)
text = text.replace(anchorA, replA, 1)
text = text.replace(anchorB, replB, 1)

with io.open(path, 'w', encoding='utf-8') as fh:
    fh.write(text)
try:
    py_compile.compile(path, cfile='/tmp/.hp758-check.pyc', doraise=True)
except Exception as exc:
    sys.stderr.write("provision: FATAL #758: patched ShellScriptlets.py does not "
        "compile: %s: %s\n" % (type(exc).__name__, exc))
    sys.exit(1)
print("provision: #758 packageEntries batched")
PY

log "writing $CONF"
cat > "$CONF" <<EOF
TREE_PATH="$PORTS_DIR"
PACKAGER="$PACKAGER"
TARGET_ARCHITECTURE="$TARGET_ARCH"
ALLOW_UNTESTED="yes"
ALLOW_UNSAFE_SOURCES="yes"
EOF

# Point haikuporter's source-mirror fallback at the DeBeOS-owned mirror (issue
# #172). Without this line haikuporter uses its compiled-in default,
# https://ports-mirror.haiku-os.org, which is permanently NXDOMAIN -- so for any
# port whose primary upstream URL has rotated, the fallback resolves nothing and
# the fetch dies. haikuporter appends the fallback as
# "$DOWNLOAD_MIRROR/<recipeDir>/<sourceBasename>" (HaikuPorter/Source.py), which
# is exactly the layout haiku-mirror-sync / the nativebuild mirror-promote step
# write to. Omitted when unset so a bare provision keeps upstream-only behaviour.
if [ -n "$DOWNLOAD_MIRROR" ]; then
	log "haikuports.conf: DOWNLOAD_MIRROR=$DOWNLOAD_MIRROR (#172)"
	printf 'DOWNLOAD_MIRROR="%s"\n' "$DOWNLOAD_MIRROR" >> "$CONF"
fi

# Put `haikuporter` on PATH as a bare command. It is a git clone invoked as
# `python3.14 $HP_DIR/haikuporter`, but the build driver (haiku-nativebuild) and
# the baked builder-AMI both expect a bare `haikuporter` on PATH -- so a lean box
# provisioned here otherwise fails "haikuporter: command not found". The wrapper
# also pins --config so every invocation resolves the arm64 haikuports.conf
# regardless of CWD (haikuporter's default config search does not find $CONF when
# the driver cd's into the ports tree).
#
# It also appends $CROSS_TOOLS_BIN to PATH. haikuporter copies its OWN PATH into
# the in-chroot build shell (filteredEnvironment() -> shellEnv['PATH'] in
# Port.py), so this is the choke point that carries the arm64 triplet compilers
# into every BUILD() -- making make's built-in aarch64-unknown-haiku-g++/-gcc
# defaults resolve without a per-recipe CC/CXX override (#186, #174; the
# Makefile-Engine ports were narrowly fixed by #182, this fixes the raw-Makefile
# class). APPENDED, not prepended, so the unprefixed native cc/gcc (in
# /boot/system/bin, earlier on PATH) and any explicit recipe CC/CXX still win.
log "installing haikuporter wrapper -> $SHIM_DIR/haikuporter"
cat > "$SHIM_DIR/haikuporter" <<EOF
#!/bin/sh
# haiku-provision-native-builder: bare-command wrapper for the haikuporter clone.
PATH="\$PATH:$CROSS_TOOLS_BIN"; export PATH
exec python3.14 "$HP_DIR/haikuporter" --config="$CONF" "\$@"
EOF
chmod 755 "$SHIM_DIR/haikuporter"

# --- 2b. chain-planning tool launchers (depclosure / haiku-package-closure) ---
# The build-wave planner runs depclosure.py to compute the authoritative,
# dependency-ordered build chain. Three things about a native Haiku builder break
# a naive invocation, and each cost a wave that "could not compute chains":
#
#   1. env-shebang. Both tools start `#!/usr/bin/env python3`, but there is no
#      /usr/bin/env on Haiku (/ is an in-memory rootfs with only /bin, /boot,
#      /dev... -- no /usr, and it cannot be baked into an AMI since the snapshot
#      is only the BFS /boot volume). `python3` itself IS on PATH (the python3.14
#      package ships /boot/system/bin/python3), but the wave's SSM shell exports an
#      empty PATH to children, so even `/bin/env python3` fails "python3: No such
#      file or directory" -- which surfaced as the wave's "no python3 on PATH".
#      The launchers sidestep all of it by exec'ing python3.14 by absolute path.
#   2. --repo default. depclosure.py defaults --repo to /opt/haiku/haikuports,
#      which does not exist here (the tree is $PORTS_DIR) -- so a bare run reads an
#      empty/absent repository/ dir and reports "0 DependencyInfo". The dependency
#      infos ARE present ($PORTS_DIR/repository, warmed at bake); the default just
#      pointed elsewhere. The launcher pins --repo "$PORTS_DIR".
#   3. --built base. depclosure saturates from the hpkgs it is told are built. The
#      toolchain (gcc, binutils, make, haiku_devel, ...) is INSTALLED from the repo,
#      not built from a recipe, so without crediting the installed package pools
#      every port reads UNREACHABLE and the plan is useless. The launcher credits
#      the system pool + the user pool + this wave's freshly built packages/, so the
#      saturation reflects the builder's actual state.
#
# Invoke bare with $SHIM_DIR on PATH (as haiku-nativebuild already does) or by
# absolute path, e.g. "$SHIM_DIR/depclosure" <target>...  -- no PATH/env needed.
log "installing depclosure launcher -> $SHIM_DIR/depclosure (src: $DEPCLOSURE_SRC)"
cat > "$SHIM_DIR/depclosure" <<EOF
#!/bin/sh
# haiku-provision-native-builder: launcher for the DeBeOS build-order tool.
# Runs python3.14 directly (the tool's #!/usr/bin/env python3 shebang cannot
# resolve on Haiku) and pins the repo + the installed/built package pools so the
# saturation is computed against this builder's real state. Extra args pass through.
exec python3.14 "$DEPCLOSURE_SRC" \\
  --repo "$PORTS_DIR" \\
  --built /boot/system/packages \\
  --built "$HP_HOME/config/packages" \\
  --built "$PORTS_DIR/packages" \\
  "\$@"
EOF
chmod 755 "$SHIM_DIR/depclosure"

log "installing haiku-package-closure launcher -> $SHIM_DIR/haiku-package-closure (src: $PKGCLOSURE_SRC)"
cat > "$SHIM_DIR/haiku-package-closure" <<EOF
#!/bin/sh
# haiku-provision-native-builder: launcher for the DeBeOS pool-closure tool.
# Runs python3.14 directly (same env-shebang reason as depclosure) and defaults
# --package-tool to the base image's \`package\` binary. Pass --pool/--base/--want
# (and any other args) through; an explicit --package-tool overrides the default.
case " \$* " in
  *" --package-tool "*) exec python3.14 "$PKGCLOSURE_SRC" "\$@" ;;
  *) exec python3.14 "$PKGCLOSURE_SRC" --package-tool /boot/system/bin/package "\$@" ;;
esac
EOF
chmod 755 "$SHIM_DIR/haiku-package-closure"

# --- 3. the fonts dir haikuporter's chroot copy assumes ------------------
if [ ! -d /boot/system/settings/fonts ]; then
	log "creating /boot/system/settings/fonts (haikuporter chroot copies it)"
	mkdir -p /boot/system/settings/fonts
fi

# --- 4. shared fetch helper + wget/curl shims that delegate to it -----------
mkdir -p "$SHIM_DIR" "$SEED_DIR"

# Resolve curl and python NOW, by absolute path, before the curl shim below
# shadows curl on PATH. This script runs without SHIM_DIR on PATH, so
# `command -v` yields the system binaries; guard curl anyway in case a prior
# run's shim is on PATH. The helper and shims must NOT rely on PATH: Haiku has
# no /usr/bin/env and the build-wave's SSM shell exports an empty PATH, so every
# tool the shims call (curl, python, the SSM agent) is invoked by absolute path.
REAL_CURL="$(command -v curl 2>/dev/null || true)"
case "$REAL_CURL" in
	"$SHIM_DIR"/*|"") REAL_CURL="/boot/system/bin/curl" ;;
esac
REAL_PY="$(command -v python3.14 2>/dev/null || echo /boot/system/bin/python3.14)"
# The S3 client for the shared source cache, resolved the same way and allowed to
# come out empty (issue 728). Since the #302 migration a canonical image ships
# only the real amazon-ssm-agent, which has no `s3` verbs, so on most boxes this
# is genuinely absent and the helper must run with its S3 tier off instead of
# baking a dead absolute path. debeos-aws first: it is the designed replacement
# and the one to find here once it is packaged into the image.
SRCCACHE_AGENT=""
for _c in /boot/system/bin/debeos-aws /boot/system/bin/debeos-ssm-agent; do
	[ -x "$_c" ] && { SRCCACHE_AGENT="$_c"; break; }
done
if [ -n "$SRCCACHE_AGENT" ]; then
	log "source-cache S3 client: $SRCCACHE_AGENT"
else
	log "source-cache S3 client: NONE on this image (issue 728) -- the issue #162"
	log "  shared cache tier will be off; seed and upstream fetches are unaffected"
fi
HELPER="$SHIM_DIR/haiku-srccache-fetch"

# The fetch helper: seed -> shared S3 cache -> upstream (then store to S3). The
# provision-time-resolved paths go in a small interpolated header; the logic is
# a verbatim (quoted-heredoc) body, so runtime $vars and the python snippet need
# no escaping. See the section 4 comment above for the S3 rationale (issue #162).
log "installing source-cache helper -> $HELPER (S3: ${SRCCACHE_S3:-off})"
cat > "$HELPER" <<HEADER
#!/bin/sh
# haiku-srccache-fetch <url> <output-path>  (installed by
# haiku-provision-native-builder; issue #162). DO NOT edit on the box -- edit the
# provisioner. Provision-time paths:
REAL_CURL="$REAL_CURL"
REAL_PY="$REAL_PY"
SEED_DIR="$SEED_DIR"
SRCCACHE_S3="$SRCCACHE_S3"
# Resolved at provision time, and may legitimately be empty (issue 728): the
# image has carried no S3 client since the #302 agent migration replaced the
# interim debeos-ssm-agent with the real amazon-ssm-agent, which has no \`s3\`
# verbs. The body below treats an empty AGENT as "S3 tier off" and says so once,
# rather than silently skipping the shared cache on every single fetch -- the
# local seed and upstream legs are unaffected either way.
AGENT="$SRCCACHE_AGENT"
HEADER
cat >> "$HELPER" <<'BODY'
#
# Shared S3-backed source-tarball cache for the native-EC2 builder fleet. The
# wget/curl shims delegate here so every SOURCE fetch goes: local seed -> shared
# S3 cache -> upstream (then store to S3). Ephemeral spot builders thus stop
# re-downloading the same tarballs across waves, and a warm S3 mirror survives a
# dead / rate-limited upstream mid-wave.
#
# Correctness, carried over from haiku-source-proxy: a fetch is written to a
# .part file and moved into place only after curl reports success AND the file
# is non-empty. `curl -f` fails on HTTP >=400 and exits nonzero on a short read
# against an advertised Content-Length, so a truncated download never becomes
# the output -- and, crucially, is never uploaded. The S3 store step runs only
# on a fully verified file, so a poisoned/short entry can never enter the shared
# cache and hand every later build a false checksum mismatch. A store FAILURE is
# non-fatal (the build already has a good file); a store of a BAD file cannot
# happen. Every fetch logs one SEED/HIT/MISS/STORE/FAIL line so silence is never
# read as success.
set -u

url="${1:-}"; out="${2:-}"
[ -n "$url" ] || { echo "haiku-srccache-fetch: no URL given" >&2; exit 2; }
# The tarball's bare filename (strip any ?query / #fragment), for the seed dir
# and for the human-readable tail of the S3 key.
f=$(basename "$url"); f=${f%%\?*}; f=${f%%#*}
[ -n "$out" ] || out="$f"
obj=""

# 1. local seed -- a hand-dropped tarball wins outright (bypasses a flaky upstream).
if [ -n "$f" ] && [ -f "$SEED_DIR/$f" ]; then
	cp "$SEED_DIR/$f" "$out" && { echo "[srccache] SEED $f"; exit 0; }
fi

# Key the S3 object on sha256(url) + a sanitized basename -- identical to
# haiku-source-proxy's cache_path, so two URLs never collide and the object is
# still recognizable at a glance. Computed with python (a guaranteed builder
# dependency) by absolute path.
# An empty AGENT means this image has no S3 client at all (issue 728), so the
# shared tier cannot be read OR written and there is no point computing a key:
# skip straight to upstream rather than letting every fetch burn an exec that
# fails 127 into /dev/null.
if [ -n "$SRCCACHE_S3" ] && [ -n "$AGENT" ]; then
	key=$("$REAL_PY" - "$url" <<'PY'
import hashlib, os, re, sys, urllib.parse
url = sys.argv[1]
digest = hashlib.sha256(url.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()
base = os.path.basename(urllib.parse.urlsplit(url).path) or 'download'
base = re.sub(r'[^A-Za-z0-9._+-]', '_', base)[:96]
print('%s-%s' % (digest, base))
PY
	)
	[ -n "$key" ] && obj="${SRCCACHE_S3%/}/$key"
fi

# 2. shared S3 cache -- serve on hit. A missing object (or an empty download)
# reads as a miss and falls through to upstream.
if [ -n "$obj" ]; then
	if "$AGENT" s3 cp "$obj" "$out.s3part" >/dev/null 2>&1 && [ -s "$out.s3part" ]; then
		mv "$out.s3part" "$out"
		echo "[srccache] HIT $key ($(wc -c < "$out" | tr -d ' ') bytes)"
		exit 0
	fi
	rm -f "$out.s3part"
fi

# 3. upstream miss -- fetch, verify, rename atomically, then store the verified
# file to S3. A failed or truncated fetch leaves no output and caches nothing.
if "$REAL_CURL" -fL --retry 3 -o "$out.part" "$url" && [ -s "$out.part" ]; then
	mv "$out.part" "$out"
	echo "[srccache] MISS $url ($(wc -c < "$out" | tr -d ' ') bytes)"
	if [ -n "$obj" ]; then
		if "$AGENT" s3 cp "$out" "$obj" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
			echo "[srccache] STORE $key"
		else
			echo "[srccache] store-failed $key (non-fatal)" >&2
		fi
	fi
	exit 0
fi

rm -f "$out.part"
echo "[srccache] FAIL $url (upstream fetch failed or truncated; cached nothing)" >&2
exit 1
BODY
chmod 755 "$HELPER"

log "installing wget shim -> $SHIM_DIR/wget (delegates to haiku-srccache-fetch)"
cat > "$SHIM_DIR/wget" <<EOF
#!/bin/sh
# haiku-provision-native-builder wget shim: haikuporter's downloader shells out
# to \`wget -O <target> <url>\`. Parse the target + URL and hand off to the shared
# source-cache helper (seed -> S3 -> upstream). Unknown flags are ignored.
url=""; out=""
while [ \$# -gt 0 ]; do case "\$1" in
  -O) out="\$2"; shift 2;; --output-document=*) out="\${1#*=}"; shift;;
  --*=*) shift;; -*) shift;; *) url="\$1"; shift;; esac; done
exec "$HELPER" "\$url" "\$out"
EOF
chmod 755 "$SHIM_DIR/wget"

log "installing curl shim -> $SHIM_DIR/curl (delegates; real curl: $REAL_CURL)"
cat > "$SHIM_DIR/curl" <<EOF
#!/bin/sh
# haiku-provision-native-builder curl shim: mirror of the wget shim for the
# recipes that fetch with curl. Parse curl's -o/-O output and the positional URL
# -- skipping the options that consume the next argument (e.g. the N in
# --retry N) so their value is never mistaken for the URL -- then hand off to the
# shared source-cache helper. With no URL this is not a fetch (e.g.
# \`curl --version\`), so pass the original argv straight to the real curl.
url=""; out=""
while [ \$# -gt 0 ]; do case "\$1" in
  -o|--output) out="\$2"; shift 2;;
  --output=*) out="\${1#*=}"; shift;;
  -O|--remote-name|--remote-name-all) shift;;
  --retry|--retry-delay|--retry-max-time|--connect-timeout|--max-time|\\
  -H|--header|-b|--cookie|-d|--data|-u|--user|-A|--user-agent|-e|--referer|\\
  -x|--proxy|-C|--continue-at|-T|--upload-file|-K|--config) shift 2;;
  --*=*) shift;; -*) shift;; *) url="\$1"; shift;; esac; done
[ -n "\$url" ] || exec "$REAL_CURL" "\$@"
exec "$HELPER" "\$url" "\$out"
EOF
chmod 755 "$SHIM_DIR/curl"

case ":$PATH:" in
	*":$SHIM_DIR:"*) : ;;
	*) log "NOTE: add $SHIM_DIR to PATH (login shells do; export it for non-login)";;
esac

cat <<EOF

provision: done. This box is a native arm64 package builder.
  ports tree : $PORTS_DIR
  haikuporter: $HP_DIR
  config     : $CONF
  seed dir   : $SEED_DIR  (drop upstream tarballs here to bypass a flaky fetch)
  src cache  : ${SRCCACHE_S3:-off}  (shared S3 tier via $SHIM_DIR/haiku-srccache-fetch)
  dl mirror  : ${DOWNLOAD_MIRROR:-off}  (haikuports.conf DOWNLOAD_MIRROR fallback, #172)
  recipe overlay: $([ "$OVERLAY_SKIP" = 1 ] && echo "SKIPPED -- this tree has NONE of the project's arm64 recipe fixes (#764)" || echo "$OVERLAY_DIR applied to the ports tree (#764)")
  chain plan : $SHIM_DIR/depclosure, $SHIM_DIR/haiku-package-closure
               (launchers; source deployed to $HP_HOME by haiku-bake-builder)

Compute a dependency-ordered build chain (no PATH/env setup needed):
  $SHIM_DIR/depclosure <port>...

Build a port (build-requires are covered by --all-dependencies):
  export PATH=$SHIM_DIR:\$PATH
  cd $HP_DIR && python3.14 ./haikuporter --config=$CONF -y --all-dependencies <port>

Publish the resulting .hpkg to the DeBeOS repo from a Haiku host:
  graviton/scripts/haiku-repo-add   (see its header for HG_* env)
EOF
